## Sequenced (post-v1)

Nothing here is deferred to a v2 that never arrives. Every item is accepted
work with a wave; a wave is a **dependency and value ordering, not a
schedule** — no dates, no durations, no estimates. Work starts on a wave
when the prior wave's blocking items are closed and Round 6 is shut.
IDs are stable: review rounds and PRDs cite them, so they are never
renumbered, only re-waved.

- **Wave 0 — ontology compatibility.** How two components decide they may
  exchange data at all. It comes first because Wave 1 moves the fingerprint
  whether or not anyone planned for it, and Wave 0 is the difference between a
  managed change and a fleet-wide surprise. `OC-PRD`.
- **Wave 1 — ontology verbs and modularity** (complete). The pack seam
  became a product: typed domain verbs, a second pack, and the seam set
  that keeps both honest. Everything else reads better once this lands.
- **Wave 2 — data breadth** (D21 complete, D18 and D13 in; D19/D1/PX4/D17
  remain, each gated on an external input). The claim that matters most
  and the one the repo is weakest on: point Exocortex at data that is not
  a coding session. Structured-source adapters plus the deterministic
  half of entity resolution, which is what makes a graph of documents
  into a model of a world — the deterministic half shipped and the
  adapter contract that governs the rest is complete; the SaaS/table
  adapters and the docs run remain.
- **Wave 3 — surface and operability** (D23, D7, D22 in; PX5, D24, D6,
  D20 remain). Make the graph visible and the node observable.
- **Wave 4 — scale.** Prove the numbers past 100k before anyone bets an
  org on them.
- **Wave 5 — multi-tenant and federated.** The capabilities that only
  matter once more than one org depends on a deployment.

