| D18 | **2** | *(D21 steps a+d)* **Git-history adapter** — commits, authors, files, PRs into `Fix` / `Problem` / `CodePattern` / `Technology`, which `exocortex-pack-dev-v1` already declares. No auth, no network, fully deterministic, offline-testable, and self-evidencing: it runs against this repository. Cheapest real adapter available and the strongest zero-friction demo, so it lands ahead of D1 | adapter roadmap; §18.4 | **Done** — `crates/exocortex-adapter-git`: `git log --format` transcription (unit-separator record format, malformed records skipped-and-counted, never guessed); `fix:`→`Fix` / else `Command` (a prefix test); `FileContext` per changed path + `Modifies` edges (the one factual relationship; PR/issue joins are D19's, not text guessing — recorded); ExternalKey identity (blake3 repo-scoped table uuid, sha/path pks) ⇒ idempotent re-runs with content-bound batch ids; D21 projection declared (window bound stops before wire traffic); binary with env-only secrets; hermetic fixture-repo suite `tests/git_history.rs` (real git binary, fixed identities/timestamps) |
| D19 | **2** | *(D21 steps a+d)* **SaaS-API adapter family** — Linear/Jira first, then GitHub, then Salesforce/ServiceNow. Highest semantic density of any source: these systems already carry relationships (issue→assignee→team, PR→commit→file), so edges are transcribed rather than inferred. Linear and GitHub join directly to D18's git graph on the same entities | adapter roadmap; §18.4 |
| D20 | **3** | *(D21 steps a+d)* **CDC adapters** — Postgres logical replication first, then MySQL binlog / Debezium. Structurally different from table sources: continuous rather than batch-with-cursor, deletes arrive as first-class events (a clean match for soft-delete and `valid_until`), primary keys map onto `ExternalKey`, and source ordering must reconcile with LSN assignment. This is the category that makes integration of *operational* systems real | adapter roadmap; §18.4 |
| D21 | **2** | **Adapter contract** — `docs/prd/adapter-contract-prd.md`. Three gaps that D1/D18-D20 all hit and none of them owns: (a) **projection discipline** — a table can hold a billion rows against a 100k benchmark, so an adapter ingests a bounded projection, never a table, and the contract must say so; (b) **local validation vs R-I1/R-I4** — the SDK links wire only and never the kernel, so rejections are discovered at the server, which is fine for 70 docs pages and untenable at table volume; needs either a server-side dry-run generalizing `preflight_wrapup` or the rulebook shipped to adapters as data; (c) **source-schema evolution policy** — `schema_hash` and `mapping_version` model it, nothing defines behaviour when a column is added or retyped. Blocks D1, D19, D20 — those three should not ship before its steps (a) projection and (d) schema policy land, or each invents its own answer and the three disagree. Its rulebook-as-data step depends on OC-PRD Wave 0, because a validation manifest is only verifiable if it is stamped with the compatibility fingerprint | `docs/prd/adapter-contract-prd.md` | **Done (all five steps)** — b: `preflight_batch` pub core on IngestServer (admit/registered_source/validate_batch, stopped before commit; embedding moved out of validate_batch — verdicts unaffected) + the `Preflight` tonic RPC (submit-identical verdicts pinned by `preflight.rs::preflight_verdicts_match_submit_verdicts_row_for_row`; commits-nothing/no-LSN-consumed/no-idempotency-claim pinned by `preflight_commits_nothing_and_leaves_no_idempotency_claim`) + the ops-registered `preflight_batch` operation (OpContext.ingest_preflight handle; the server stamps the REAL registration ceiling/fingerprint and signs server-side as the registered producer; MCP and HTTP byte-identical parity in `http_parity.rs::preflight_batch_answers_identically_over_http_and_the_registry`; standalone fails loudly) + SDK `AdapterSession::preflight` (the shared `prepare_batches` path; zero session-state mutation); c: the wire `manifest` module + `GetValidationManifest` RPC (compiled from OntologySummary: type-name-to-id, kinds with computed-only markers, triples, title char bounds, the REGISTERED ceiling; scheme-checked reader), the SDK interpreter (`manifest.rs`; submit_window and preflight validate locally first when a manifest is held; stale-fingerprint/unparseable/absent manifests degrade to server-side validation with a warning), golden parity `manifest_parity.rs::manifest_verdicts_agree_row_for_row` (every locally-computable RejectCode class, both paths); e: `cargo xtask adapter-contract` (13 obligation canaries across projection/bounds/preflight/manifest/schema-policy + the adapter-crate bijection; negative fixture in the xtask suite; listed in AGENTS.md). Recorded boundaries: preflight does not consult the idempotency registry (replay is a transport property, not a data verdict — a preflighted batch_id submits fresh); `assigned_lsn` is always 0 and `similar_to` always empty on dry runs; the registry face signs server-side (an authenticated principal impersonating the registered producer, nothing commits); cross-batch `to_memory_id` verdicts are server-only and honestly skipped by the local interpreter; earlier a+d boundaries unchanged. a+d detail:  (a) `ProjectionDescriptor` on `RegisterSource` + `AdapterConfig.projection` (table flavors require; registration validates mapped-fields-⊆-schema; changes need a `mapping_version` bump); SDK window/run bound enforcement + `SourceRewound` rewind detection (`adapter-sdk/tests/projection.rs`); (d) the D4 verdict table at re-registration (`schema_evolution.rs`: unmapped-add → accept + exactly one audit row via the new `append_audit` seam; remove/retype/rename → fail closed) and submit-time `PROJECTION_BOUND_EXCEEDED`/`SCHEMA_DRIFT`/`SOURCE_REWOUND` (wire codes 15-17 with corrections + playbook rows; reject manifest extended). Recorded boundaries: session/custom/docs flavors exempt in v1 (Mintlify migration tracked); graph-share evaluation deferred to Dreams (PRD open question 1); snapshot history in memory (D20 owns durability); the declared schema is adapter-authored, not source-introspected. Steps b (PreflightBatch), c (manifest), e (gate) tracked as ac2/ac3/ac5 |
| D22 | **3** | **Training-corpus export** — materialize the graph as JSONL for training and evaluation. Two properties fall out of the existing design rather than needing to be built: bi-temporal validity yields **temporally-clean splits** (everything the graph believed as of time T, with no future leakage), and provenance plus `ExternalKey` plus the audit ledger yield a **per-record lineage manifest** answering where each row came from. Dreams' MCR² and `SimilarTo` machinery already covers corpus dedup. Adjacent to the shipped `--export` work | training direction (below) | **Done** — `server/src/corpus_export.rs` + `--export-corpus <dir> [--corpus-as-of RFC3339]` on the node (one-shot, falkor, the org-backup pattern): `memories.jsonl`/`edges.jsonl`/`lineage.jsonl`/`manifest.json`, mode-0600 atomic writes. The cut is bi-temporal by construction (`recorded_at <= T && valid_from <= T && valid_until open or > T`); edges export only when BOTH endpoints made the cut (no dangling rows); lineage carries provenance kind, author/rule/source_uri, producer, RAW external coordinates (R-T18a, straight from snapshot provenance), entity ids, LSN; the manifest names the compatibility fingerprint, the cut, the computed-only kinds present in the edge cut (drop Dreams rows by kind), and the D24 egress boundary on every write. `corpus_export.rs::corpus_cut_is_temporally_clean_and_carries_lineage` pins the temporal table row by row |
| D23 | **3** | **LLM boundary decision — RESOLVED as (a), LLM as an external producer.** The `no LLM inside` invariant (R-D6/CR-19, `cargo xtask no-llm`, AGENTS.md rule 1) stands unchanged and CI-enforced: extraction runs out-of-process in adapter repos (the gate greps THIS workspace, not adapter repos), emits typed drafts through the same signed ingest path, and is stamped with the distinguishing `PRODUCER_KIND_EXTRACTED` (wire enum 6, additive; `ProducerKind::Extracted` kernel-side) so its rows are distinguishable as a class in provenance and filterable/revocable at query time. Option (b) — LLM inside the node — remains an explicit invariant reversal requiring a record here and in `docs/MILESTONE_REPORT.md`; no such reversal is recorded | direction decision | **Done** — decision recorded here and in `docs/MILESTONE_REPORT.md`; made executable by the additive `Extracted` producer kind end to end (wire enum, kernel enum, ingest mapping) with the stamping pinned by `ingest.rs::extraction_producers_are_stamped_distinguishably`; older servers reject the new kind fail-closed (correct rolling-upgrade behavior). The query-side filter surface ships with the first real extraction adapter, not before (recorded boundary: distinguishability is stored data; a filter over it is the adapter's acceptance, not this decision's) |
| D24 | **3** | **Rights and consent provenance** — if corpora leave the system for training, "may this be trained on" becomes a first-class field: licence, consent basis, retention limit, redaction state, per source and per record. Not modelled today. Gates D22 for any corpus that leaves the org | training direction (below) |
| D25 | **1** | **Name the change-feed seam (`ChangeLog`)** — the one major subsystem with no written contract. Append-at-LSN lives in `cluster/src/node.rs`, feed mount and auth in `server/src/sse.rs`, consumption and gap detection in `client/src/sync.rs`: three crates holding three partial ideas of one contract, which is exactly how CS1 happened (an empty replay ring answering "you are current" instead of 409 Resync, silently dropping every invalidation in the gap). Ships as a trait with four operations over today's in-memory ring — no behaviour change, no new dependency, no fingerprint move — plus a conformance suite landing as the seventh seam in PX3's inventory. **The case is CS1, not scale** | palantir-expansion PRD §3.3 S7 | **Done** — `cluster/src/change_log.rs`: the `ChangeLog` trait (append / replay_since / replay_floor / frontier) over the moved `RingChangeLog`; `ClusterNode` appends through the seam and exposes `&dyn ChangeLog`, the SSE handler replays through it, and `Replay` lives with the contract; conformance suite `cluster/tests/change_log.rs` pins LSN order, floor-vs-frontier truth, the CS1 empty-log rule, CS8 saturation, and dyn-safety — the seventh seam for PX3's inventory. No behaviour change, no new dependency, no fingerprint move |
| D26 | **4** | **Broker-backed change log** — swap a durable, replayable broker in behind D25's port. **Redpanda** as the reference (single binary, Kafka-wire-compatible, no JVM and no ZooKeeper, so it sits with the single-binary ethos rather than against it); NATS JetStream the alternative. Apache Kafka itself is rejected: the operational surface contradicts the product's own deployment claim. Two things must be decided before any implementation, not during: **(a) LSN partitioning** — `next_lsn_block` hands out a contiguous, globally ordered range and the client's `since_lsn` resync depends on that order being real, while a partitioned log orders only within a partition; a single partition is almost certainly right at this tier and must be written down, because the pressure to "just add partitions" arrives under load and not while reading a PRD. **(b) The in-process implementation stays the default forever** — a broker is opt-in, or standalone mode and the single-binary claim both die. Gated on genuine scale pressure or on the D22 training direction being accepted, whichever comes first | D25; D22; D14 |
